    "summary": "Scarlet Begonias fires, strong second set, first‑set uneven",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a scorching Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain sequence and an inspired Estimated Prophet that leads into a memorable Other One sandwich. Jerry’s solo shines, Brent’s keyboards blaze, while the first set feels a bit chaotic.",
    "review": "On March 26, 1983 at the Aladdin Theater in Las Vegas, the Dead served up a night of high‑octane improvisation tempered by a shaky opening set. The first half stumbled through a jumbled mix of classics—Jack Straw, Friend Of The Devil and a surprisingly odd My Brother Esau that some listeners found lackluster—but it set the stage for a second set that truly took off. Scarlet Begonias launched into Fire on the Mountain with Phil “dropping the bombs,” and Brent’s keyboards ignited the ensuing Estimated Prophet, creating a soaring groove that carried straight into an expansive Other One sandwich capped by a heartfelt Stella Blue. Jerry’s beautifully phrased solo stands out as a highlight, and his vocals remain crisp throughout. The encore’s Touch of Grey rounds out a night that, despite its uneven start, offers several standout moments for any Deadhead collector.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest window into this performance, the soundboard capture (gd1983-03-26.140315.sbd.eaton.miller.clugston.flac1648) delivers crystal‑clear audio that showcases every nuance—from Phil’s thunderous low end to Brent’s fiery keys—making it the definitive listening choice.",
